<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Powys Wenwynwyn</h1><p class="st-meta">1160 CE – 1283 CE (approx.) · capital Welshpool</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern half of Powys, ruled from Welshpool by princes who often sided with England against Gwynedd.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd's</a> death in 1160, his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-cyfeiliog">Owain Cyfeiliog</a> took the southern part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Powys</a>. It was named after Owain's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwenwynwyn-ab-owain">Gwenwynwyn ab Owain</a>, who ruled from 1195 and tried to lead the Welsh princes himself.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwenwynwyn-ab-owain">Gwenwynwyn</a>'s rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Gwynedd</a> cost him his lands for long periods. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-gwenwynwyn">Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn</a> fought and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> in turn, and plotted against him in 1274. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-cyfeiliog">Owain Cyfeiliog</a> was a noted poet and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strata-marcella-abbey">Strata Marcella Abbey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welshpool">Welshpool</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/powis-castle">Powis Castle</a> were its centres.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Gruffydd supported Edward I in the conquest of 1282 to 1283, and his son Owain surrendered the princely title, holding the lands as an English barony. The heiress married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-charlton-1st-baron-charlton">John Charlton</a>, and the lordship of Powis passed to his family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/powys-wenwynwyn.jpg" alt="Powys Wenwynwyn" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Powys.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1160 CE – 1283 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Welshpool</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Owain Cyfeiliog, Gwenwynwyn ab Owain, Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Powys, Powys Cyfeiliog</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1222&amp;focus=powys-wenwynwyn" aria-label="Powys Wenwynwyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4609 362 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Powys Wenwynwyn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4929 618L4910 602M4929 618L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 602h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4929 618h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4929 618h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Welshpool. <a href="/atlas?year=1222&amp;focus=powys-wenwynwyn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
